{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"73651a459f29dd7450c6c7cc39df246c43793a49"},"timestamp":1536874040,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTE1ODkwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421158902"},"message":"[GitMate.io](https://gitmate.io) thinks possibly related issues are https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/18 (git-annex diff), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2328 (--all (or just --git-annex-options?) for datalad drop), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1974 (Datalad adds to annex files that were already in git), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2648 (Git annex init fail on /// from datalad.org on Windows), and https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/451 (git annex -J2 for content to come from datalad-archives fails).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536894561,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTIxNzI5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421217297"},"message":"I think I've answered at least some aspects in other, more focused questions, so let me just give a quick answer to the main question:\n\n**how do I create my own superdataset?**\n\nSee our definition for the superdataset in the [glossary](http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/glossary.html) and then the answer is\n- step 1: **create  (or take existing) a dataset** --  `datalad create mysuperdatasetlocation`\n- step 2: you have choices.  Assuming that you did `cd mysuperdatasetlocation` you could\n  - **create a new subdataset**, which would in effect finally make your current dataset a \"superdataset\" -- `datalad create -d . myfirstsub`\n  - **install an existing dataset as a subdataset of this one** -- `datalad install -d . URL` (e.g. `datalad install -d . ///openfmri/ds000001` to make ds000001 a subdataset of your current ont)\n  - **move some existing repository under your new superdataset, and add it as a subdataset** -- `datalad add -d . mysubrepo` (note that it must not be `--bare`, see #2820 )\n\nsomething like this .\n\nAs for the rest, consider\n- git-annex is an addition to git to version control largefiles within git\n- datalad is an addition to git and (optionally) git-annex, to \n   - make it easier to operate on hierarchies/collections of datasets somewhat bluring the boundaries between them\n     - many operations are recursive (install, get, ...)\n     - you can specify full path within some (sub)dataset and we invoke git/git-annex under appropriate path\n   - make it easy to automatically create and update datasets from existing websites/servers (that [`crawler` extension](https://github.com/datalad/datalad-crawler/))\n   - take more advantages from git/git-annex as for e.g.\n     - recording provenance of commands execution and being able to replay them, see [datalad run/rerun](http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/generated/man/datalad-run.html)\n     - https://github.com/datalad/datalad-container\n     - ...\n\nWE NEED TO WRITE THAT DAMN PAPER PRESENTING DATALAD to the masses.  Your questions are great source of ideas for what questions that we need to answer with it ;-)","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536894574,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NDUxMTQ1MzA="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1536903693,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTIzNzE4MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421237181"},"message":"Here is my take on your core questions.\n\n\u003e What is the relationship to git annex?\n\nGreat! DataLad uses git-annex for file content tracking and transport logistics. You already seem to have a good idea what git-annex is, so I will just say that it offers an astonishing range of functionality that DataLad tries to expose in full.\n\nApart from that there is one major difference: Both git and git annex are made to work with a single repository at a time. Even things like git submodules (that DataLad uses internally) do not change this much (if you doubt that, try to find out what you need to do to bring a hierarchy of submodules back into a clean state after placing a new file in a random subdirectory). One of the key advantages that DataLad brings to the table is that it tries really hard to make the boundaries between repositories vanish from a user's point of via. Most core commands have a `--recursive` option that will discover and traverse any subdatasets and do-the-right-thing (TM). For example, `datalad add. --recursive` will solve the above example, no matter what was changed/added, no matter where in a tree of subdatasets.\n\nAnother useful feature is that DataLad provides users with the ability to act on \"virtual\" file paths. For example, if your software needs datafiles for a unit test, but you carry them in a Git submodule, due to size or other issues: you can simply say `datalad get somepath_to_submodule/some_path_to_file`\nand DataLad will discover if there are any subdatasets to install at a particular version to eventually provide the file content. Here is a real example from a real test:\n\nhttps://github.com/psychoinformatics-de/remodnav/blob/master/remodnav/tests/test_detect.py#L92\n\nThis line (plus the DataLad import) is all you need to make you uni test battery robust against changes in file hosting, version, etc. And it will also effectively become a caching layer, as DataLad won't download things twice if not needed. There is not need to a user how to setup and initialize the data submodule, it all happens by requesting the needed file.\n\nThat being said, apart from the algorithmic magic, DataLad absolutely minimizes the use of unique/idiosyncratic functionality/implementations, but uses standard Git/git-annex functionality to achieve its goals. If DataLad should vanish at some point in the future, you'll be an agony due to the loss in convenience, but you will loose nothing else, incl. access to local or remote resources.\n\n\u003e How does Github relate to DataLad?\n\nDataLad can make good use of Github, if you have figured out storage for your large files otherwise. On our website is a demo that shows how you can make DataLad automatically publish file content to one location and afterward push an update to Github, such that users can install directly from Github and seemingly also obtain large file content from Github -- but really it is DataLad orchestrating git-annex to perform its magic in the background: https://www.datalad.org/for/data-publication (last demo on this page).\n\nGithub is also capable of resolving submodule/subdataset links to other Github repos, which makes for a nice UI. Here is a dataset that contains retinotopic maps computed for all participants in out http://studyforrest.org project.\n\nhttps://github.com/psychoinformatics-de/studyforrest-data-retinotopy\n\nFor proper reproducibility we want to track all input data that those are computed from, and you can find them all listed here:\n\nhttps://github.com/psychoinformatics-de/studyforrest-data-retinotopy/tree/master/src\n\nOne click gets you to the exact version of the inputs that you will need to see.\n\n\u003e How do I create a superdataset?\n\nThere is no difference between a mere dataset and a superdataset. A superdataset is defined by the fact that it has subdatasets. There are two ways to add subdatasets:\n\n- create a new one: `datalad create -d . new_subds`\n- install an existing one: `datalad install -d . new_subds`\n\nIn both cases the `-d .` instructs DataLad to \"register\" the created/installed Git repo as a submodule/subdataset with the parent (identified by its path `.`).\n\nOtherwise there is no conceptual or technical difference between a dataset, subdataset, or superdataset.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1536903712,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NDUyNDYzMDE="},"added":["documentation"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536932517,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTM2MjE0Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421362146"},"message":"The `dl.get()` is really powerful! You could maintain datasets of entire testing data / functions and then just grab them when needed :)\n\n\u003e If DataLad should vanish at some point in the future, you'll be an agony due to the loss in convenience, but you will loose nothing else, incl. access to local or remote resources.\n\nThis is very important so I'm highlighting it again here.\n\n\u003e ... if you have figured out storage for your large files otherwise.\n\nOy, this is definitely a continued problem, always. \"Who pays for the storage and where is it and what do you mean it's going to take months to transfer?\"\n\nOkay definitely add a note about the superdataset just being akin to a submodule, for some reason I missed this in my reading!\n\n## Metadata\nokay so just to clarify on this - if I want to set up metadata for a dataset I would:\n\ndefine some extractor (maybe I need to write a new one even) and then add it to the `.datalad/config`\n```\n[datalad \"metadata\"]\n  nativetype = exif\n  nativetype = xmp\n```\nThat helps datalad just know what exactors to expect to use across the dataset? And I would put the `.datalad/config` at any root of a dataset (superdataset or dataset within) but within a specific folder of a dataset (or would that automatically define it as another dataset?)\n\nSo if there is some missing / not valid metadata that the extractor tries to extract, it just reports an error and moves on. And technically if I wanted validation before extraction, I could write this into my extractor? And technically, this extractor script can just be used to do anything that requires parsing over a tree of files?\n\nAnd the function that actually does this walking of the tree is the [aggregate-metadata](http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/generated/man/datalad-aggregate-metadata.html#man-datalad-aggregate-metadata). Does it  (given the listing of types in the `.datalad/config`) know how to filter, based on extension, or does it just loop through and try them all listed against all file content (so for the above example, two loops, on the first try reading in file as exif, for the second, as xmp). How does this relate to the meta.json files found in the subdirectories (are those the summaries at those particular roots, or are they generated for a specific folder or file?)\n\nAnd then once extraction runs, this means the \"summary of metadata\" is complete. Then I could run:\n\n```\ndatalad -f json metadata --get-aggregates\n```\nto get a summary of the metadata in json. I'm thinking this would be an easy way in some CI, let's say we validate a metadata submission, we could then (when merged) run this function to generate an updated tree that feeds into a visualization of the dataset on Github pages.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536936811,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTM4NDEzOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421384138"},"message":"\u003e The `dl.get()` is really powerful! You could maintain datasets of entire testing data / functions and then just grab them when needed :)\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e If DataLad should vanish at some point in the future, you'll be an agony due to the loss in convenience, but you will loose nothing else, incl. access to local or remote resources.\n\u003e \n\u003e This is very important so I'm highlighting it again here.\n\nyes, that is the beauty of distributed VCS to some degree - that you will have all the data needed to either provide alternative \"implementation\" or a conversion to some other comparable framework.\n\nDataLad itself adds little to nothing to standard git/git-annex in terms of internal structures/standards.  We rely on git submodules for composition, we rely on git-annex for data logistics.  We barely add a single config file (`.datalad/config`) with a UUID to identify dataset, which is somewhat optional.  Only extracted metadata is genuine to DataLad, but that one is just a collection of .json files - so still readable etc.\n\nDigging deeper, even if git-annex goes away, its data stored in git-annex branch is stored in very simple files, with clear [well described structure](https://git-annex.branchable.com/internals/) -- just checkout `git-annex` branch to see/navigate it.  So I could see someone providing some basic lightweight implementations if e.g. it is desired to only get the files content.\n\nSo I think you indeed should be safe on that end","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536936896,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTM4NDYxMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421384612"},"message":"\u003e Okay definitely add a note about the superdataset just being akin to a submodule, for some reason I missed this in my reading!\n\nSomewhat opposite -- the `subdataset` IS a submodule.  `superdataset` is the \"parent\" repository having that submodule.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536938121,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTM5MTIwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-421391205"},"message":"\u003e ## Metadata\n\u003e okay so just to clarify on this - if I want to set up metadata for a dataset I would:\n\u003e \n\u003e define some extractor (maybe I need to write a new one even) and then add it to the `.datalad/config`\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e [datalad \"metadata\"]\n\u003e   nativetype = exif\n\u003e   nativetype = xmp\n\u003e ```\n\u003e That helps datalad just know what exactors to expect to use across the dataset?\n\nNot \"across\" but \"within\", otherwise correct ;)\n\n\u003e  And I would put the `.datalad/config` at any root of a dataset (superdataset or dataset within) but within a specific folder of a dataset (or would that automatically define it as another dataset?)\n\nWe do not inherit those settings within subdatasets.  I.e. if `superdataset` defines those extractors, they will be applied only to that superdataset.  You will need to define the same (or different) extractors in each subdataset as well.\n\n\u003e So if there is some missing / not valid metadata that the extractor tries to extract, it just reports an error and moves on. And technically if I wanted validation before extraction, I could write this into my extractor? \n\nyes\n\n\u003e And technically, this extractor script can just be used to do anything that requires parsing over a tree of files?\n\nextractor has access to the list of files known to repository in `self.files`.  Then it could do with them whatever it wants - filter, process, whatever. See e.g. https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/master/datalad/metadata/extractors/exif.py#L47\n\n\u003e And the function that actually does this walking of the tree is the [aggregate-metadata](http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/generated/man/datalad-aggregate-metadata.html#man-datalad-aggregate-metadata). Does it (given the listing of types in the `.datalad/config`) know how to filter, based on extension, or does it just loop through and try them all listed against all file content (so for the above example, two loops, on the first try reading in file as exif, for the second, as xmp).\n\nit is up to extractor - see above referenced code.  And yes - `aggregate-metadata` traverses (if `-r`) the entire tree. Otherwise you could just run it in the super dataset with `--incremental` to absorb metadata from immediate children subdatasets.\n\n\u003e How does this relate to the meta.json files found in the subdirectories (are those the summaries at those particular roots, or are they generated for a specific folder or file?)\n\nfirst I got confused what `meta.json` we are talking about but then realized that we have not pointed you to the big change which happened in [0.10.0](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md#0100-jun-09-2018----the-release)  release:  metadata support was completely redone.\n\n- `meta.json`, which are stored under `.datalad/meta` (such as [this](http://datasets.datalad.org/.datalad/meta/meta.json)) are the **old** extracted (by pre-0.10 versions) metadata.  It provided metadata only at the level of a dataset, with an optimism of being able to harmonize it across different \"parsers\" (we called them, instead of extractors, see e.g. that [old codebase](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/tree/0.9.x/datalad/metadata/parsers)).  **Forget about those!**, although we do keep them available from http://datasets.datalad.org so older datalad versions could still perform their search etc\n- **new** (current) extracted metadata is available under `.datalad/metadata` (more documentation TODO: #2831).  Have a look e.g. at our [superdataset's](http://datasets.datalad.org/.datalad/metadata/) and look into [aggregate_v1.json](http://datasets.datalad.org/.datalad/metadata/aggregate_v1.json) which is just an index pointing to the corresponding metadata files stored under `objects` there per each path. One file `ds-` for high level dataset metadata, and `cn-` for all the gory details per each file.  Some of those files are xz compressed, so I have a little jq-based helper to view them conveniently:\n```bash\njql ()\n{   \n    # using ucat from unp package to just \"do the right thing\"\n    for f in \"$@\"; do\n        ucat \"$f\" | jq -C . | less -R\n    done\n}\n```\n\n\u003e And then once extraction runs, this means the \"summary of metadata\" is complete. Then I could run:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e datalad -f json metadata --get-aggregates\n\u003e ```\n\u003e to get a summary of the metadata in json.\n\nyeap -- pretty much that aggregate_v1.json I mentioned above.\n\n\u003e  I'm thinking this would be an easy way in some CI, let's say we validate a metadata submission, we could then (when merged) run this function to generate an updated tree that feeds into a visualization of the dataset on Github pages.\n\nsure, could be done.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266475,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzODYzMjE4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2824#issuecomment-538632186"},"message":"Closing \"question\"-type issue with a response, and no further activity.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266475,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjY4OTE4NTkwMg=="},"status":2}]}